CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ variable: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
GCC's __complex__ extension (gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Complex.html; Clang
accepts it as well): a braced list of two scalars initializes the real and
the imaginary part.  A local variable worked, a mem-initializer `v{r, i}' of
a __complex__ float member was rejected ("initialization of POD requires one
argument, but got 2", bin53; cpp_constructor now handles it).  This was the
root cause of the drops of libstdc++'s complex<float>, complex<double> and
complex<long double> constructors (complex:1352/1509/1666 with GCC 13), so
std::complex<float>(1, 2) was nondeterministic (test_std_complex.desc).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this
program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
